# Line-ending policy.
#
# Development happens on Windows, where git's core.autocrlf=true converts
# text files to CRLF on checkout. For most files that is harmless, but a
# shell script is not most files: Elastic Beanstalk executes
# .platform/hooks/**/*.sh on Amazon Linux, and a CRLF script fails at the
# shebang line — `#!/bin/bash\r` is not a program that exists. The failure
# is silent from the deploy's point of view (the hooks are written to never
# fail a deployment), so a media migration or cleanup would simply never
# run, with nothing obviously wrong.
#
# This is not hypothetical for this repo specifically: .ebignore exists, so
# `eb deploy` zips the working directory rather than using `git archive`.
# Whatever line endings a developer's checkout produced are exactly what
# ships to production.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the Procfile and the platform/ebextensions config that
# the EB platform parses on the instance.
Procfile text eol=lf
*.config text eol=lf
